00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:25.580
But if you don't care how I did this and you're okay with vibe coding and don't have moral issues with AI, you should go to the website, NostalgiaCast.com, because every episode now has so much more content for it.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:30.400
Ways to really enhance listening to the podcast again if you're one of those freaks who does that.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:40.200
Or if you don't want to listen to the podcast and you're like, I just want to remember something funny that I heard in an episode, you can use our search tab and you can search for all sorts of crazy things.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.820
Like, did Sean ever say cornballer?

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:52.240
I'm really glad that you gave the audience the option to, like, sort of morally object to this because you never asked me.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.900
I'm giving them the opportunity to opt out of the podcast, actually.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:59.040
Yeah, I didn't know, for the record, didn't know about this website until after it was already done.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:03.920
Yep. Oh, so you would have stopped it, yeah?

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:08.420
I... You're, you know, your guess is as good as mine.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:10.800
If you want to get to my house, you have to go through my house's website.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:15.800
That's what my house's website does.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:18.340
It generates keys for people to come into the house, yeah.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:22.200
Hmm. You have to have a digital key code to get into the house.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.940
Do you happen to have, like, a boxing ring in your basement?

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.700
Uh, no, but, you know, I've always wanted...

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:32.280
My uncle had a, like, one of those big boxing bags that you punch.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.920
Like a punching bag. A punching bag, yeah, but it's boxed, but it's for boxing specifically.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:39.040
Uh, that's a joke.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:40.820
Uh, it's just a punching bag.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.900
But I think, like, those are the coolest things to have in your basement.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.760
And they're so performative at the same time.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:48.340
Like, you never have to hit that thing.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:51.160
But just having one in your basement, people would think, like, wow, this guy's tough.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:54.100
I mean, I think you're tough.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.960
Right? Like, but you would think I'm even tougher if you came into my basement.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.680
Like, think about it. In my basement, I have that row machine.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:02.520
I don't need to ever row again.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:03.920
You think I'm some senator.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:11.560
I'm just glad that I gave you an opportunity to get back to the game.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:14.780
Or just, not even get back to the game, but start talking about the game.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:16.140
We don't need to talk about the game.

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:20.980
And I do need to say that usually when we don't need to talk about the game, it's because the game is bad.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:23.760
I'm not trying to say that here.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.920
I just felt like we had some good momentum going in here.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:28.640
Boxing is all about momentum.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:31.440
And if this was round one, I'm scoring us a 10.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.160
All right? Well said.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:35.080
I'm not morally opposed to your website.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:36.780
See, that's all it takes.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:40.080
It just takes a little, that's what AI is missing, is the human part.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:41.460
A little obfuscation.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:43.120
We'll work together with the AI.

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:48.180
Right, exactly. Because I'm guiding the AI, it's okay.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.400
And you should feel okay going to the website.

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:57.020
If the AI acted on its own, imagine if AI called me in the morning and said, hey, I designed a website for you.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.960
That'd be terrifying. I didn't know that's not how it happened.

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:06.740
No, yeah. I've never received a call from AI, hoping that that streak continues in 2027.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:08.000
So boxing.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:10.900
Boxing. We've got a boxing game here.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:23.640
And the thing that makes this one different, the hook, if you will, is that there is a career mode with, I'll describe it very generously as light RPG elements.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:30.180
There aren't, like, decisions you have to make in your career that, like, change or alter the path.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:38.720
There aren't, like, storyline fights or mob payouts that you have to, like, agree to take the fall in order to collect the paycheck.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:39.860
But you could headcanon that.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:40.860
You could.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:42.860
Yeah, exactly. You could make those stories up.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:58.140
There were entire years of my childhood where I made up, playing on Super Smash 64, I made up storylines for, like, Mario and Donkey Kong had a rivalry and needed to go into a 99 stock battle.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.420
And I would just commentate in my head.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:04.140
That's normal. And I just unlocked that memory for the first time in 20 years.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:07.860
It was a very lonely childhood.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:09.780
But think about it.

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:13.560
I was doing, I was basically being Jim Ross and Triple H at the same time.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:21.200
But in this one, the RPG elements are more like your speed, your stamina, your defense, and your punch.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:33.820
And you can do those, you can basically build those up and you have to build them up via either training or through the fights on the cards that you're in.

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:38.160
And it's something where you start out as basically a nobody.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.980
And I don't know, but I felt like I needed to train first.

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:49.400
And I might have trained just by doing the four train, the four different training things.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:56.540
I might have trained too hard for the very first fight in the game because I was like up compared to the guy.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:11.620
And it's not too terrible because, you know, the mechanics of training and fighting are basically identical, at least in what you're doing, not so much what you're rewarded with.

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.020
But wasn't that weird?

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:14.280
It's a little weird.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:16.680
I thought it was going to be minigames. Didn't you think you were going to do minigames, right?

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:20.080
Yeah, exactly. So I thought I had it totally wrong at the beginning.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:22.560
And I think it also led me to overtraining a bit at the beginning.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:30.960
But I thought that the box at the top where you select your training, like you, you, there's also like the regular match box underneath.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:35.660
There's a box that has your training, like the elements that you can train.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.040
And then underneath there's a box with like your, your matches.

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:42.020
But I didn't see a way to jump from one box to the other.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:43.720
So I didn't think I could.

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:49.980
I thought I had to do these trainings and then it would like tell me my rank based on that in the other box.

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:57.300
So I thought that when I pick like train speed, it was like, okay, you're just going to get speed from doing this thing, which is what you're, what you're doing.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:59.480
But I thought I was still playing a ranked match.

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:02.080
So I did that like several times.

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:05.440
And then I realized I trained a lot.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:10.360
And then I realized, oh, now I can do a ranked match with all these stats that I've accrued.

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:11.580
If that makes any sense.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:19.660
I think I have like a muscle memory of whenever I get to a menu of just seeing if select does anything.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:33.960
And that pretty quickly showed me that you just toggle between the two menus, but I can totally get, um, because just looking at it, it's not entirely clear, like the structure of these menus.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:38.700
So, uh, it sounds like you had a pretty easy fight or two after that.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:52.300
Yeah. Well, the, the first, the first flight was definitely easier, but, um, but yeah, then it did, you know, I was still encouraged or, or, uh, I was still like rewarded for going back and training more incentivized.

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:54.900
I should say. Yes. Yeah. Training is training is essential.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:09:05.860
It's not trivial. Uh, I guess it was just like, yeah, if you do, if you do train too much that, especially before you ever go into your first regular match, your regular match just winds up feeling like more training.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.740
Uh, and I think it's important for the listeners.

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:11.520
Cause you know, we've played a couple of different kinds of boxing games.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:13.260
This is not punch out.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:18.660
This isn't, um, like a pattern memorization, uh, rhythms style game.

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:28.120
This is like, um, maybe this is going far back, but like data East's ranking game, uh, is very similar to this, uh, ranking, not an episode.

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:42.720
Joe was on. So Joe, I'll forgive you if you don't remember it, but Sean, that was a memorable game because in between rounds, the, you had the, uh, the guys patching you up, but they were patching you up in your crotch area.

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:44.620
In a very, yeah. Suggestive manner.

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:48.240
Right. So it didn't look, uh, it didn't look good.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:56.980
And, um, Hey, I guess if you want to run a search on the website for blow jobs, you would get results because it would show you that we talked about it in that episode.

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:57.880
So we're talking about it again.

00:09:58.040 --> 00:09:59.660
Now, two episodes now with blow jobs.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:02.840
Um, it's closer to that kind of game.

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:05.020
So the fights are definitely more random.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:13.520
Um, everybody just kind of like has the same style of fighting in terms of AI, but then they're just like based on their stats.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:19.920
Like, obviously they have like, uh, better stamina or better spirit or, uh, harder hitting punches.

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:21.460
All those things matter.

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:23.380
And that's what takes you through it.

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:29.640
You have a ranking system, uh, where it's weirdly like it's redundant.

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:32.280
And it says like ranking one, ranking two, ranking three, ranking four.

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:38.220
I feel like it should have given them like character names like there instead of ranking one.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:43.020
Like that just feels so computer to just have them like listed like that.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:44.960
It feels way too computer.

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:46.300
If, yeah, exactly.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:49.600
It just feels like it's like, okay, like what does that mean to the human?

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:58.040
So you go to ranking five, your start at ranking six and you fight that person and then you can move on to ranking four.

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:01.420
But you can fight the person in ranking one at any time.

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:05.300
Exactly. Right. Yeah. That's the interesting part is that you could just say, you know what?

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:06.220
I'm better than this.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:13.220
I did all the training. I'm just going to go right to ranking one or just right to ranking two, whatever ranking you prefer, and that you'll move through.

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:18.360
But if you lose one of the rankings, then you go, you drop your ranking back down.

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:19.880
Not like it matters.

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:20.940
It's not like you have to restart.

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:23.100
It's not like you lose any of your stats.

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:25.500
You're just losing your placement.

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:27.160
And you feel shame. Yeah.

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:30.820
You feel, you feel the shame of losing your placement in the boxing arena.

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:36.880
Now I feel like that's basically the, the setup of, um, the career system.

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:43.560
And you just continue to do that through, um, you know, various, uh, various different ranking systems.

00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:47.380
So once you complete, uh, that first set, you just move on to another set.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:51.620
So the real meat and potatoes of the game is the boxing.

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:56.640
So I have to ask, is the boxing, like, you know, we've played boxing games.

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:57.840
We played wrestling games.

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:00.920
We've played, um, street brawlers.

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:09.680
Like it does this feel like it is getting the sport right, or does it just become a button mashing video game?

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:18.540
Well, I think compared to, and I did look up some footage of ranking just to remind myself.

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:21.780
Um, and it does feel better than that.

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:34.120
I think there's some presentation acts aspects of this game that, and I guess just like sort of quality of life where like, it actually looks like boxing a bit more.

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:38.040
Like you're kind of rotating around each other, like you would in a boxing game.

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:45.600
Whereas I feel like in ranking, it was just sort of like, you're just gonna, it's more like two dimensional.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:57.460
Um, it, uh, like the whole aspect of you getting better, like the kind of the crowd starts to fill out more as you go up these ranks.

00:12:57.760 --> 00:12:59.660
So it feels very boxing.

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:03.680
There's a playboy bunny that comes out and shows the round number.

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:06.580
That's the, that, that, that feels authentic.

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:13.880
Um, but I mean, I still think that it, it is kind of just a button masher after all that stuff.

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:14.780
Yeah. Yeah.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:16.520
I think I see it on like two levels.

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:20.760
There's like on the micro level on the moment to moment punching each other.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:24.460
There's room for improvement for sure.

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:29.740
Where like the, just like the hit boxes feel weird.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:33.700
I can't tell sometimes if I'm punching past the person or if I'm hitting the person.

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:38.080
The, it's like that feedback, which is often missing from some of these games.

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:47.140
Like they have not perfected that, but I will say that even that feels better than like a lot of other boxing slash wrestling games that we've played.

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:54.860
Like it's still like, yeah, you kind of have like you're up a down a left a right a kind of attacks that like make it.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:56.400
It's like a little simpler.

00:13:56.400 --> 00:14:02.800
I feel like a lot of these games you're like, okay, so like how many, what like passcode do I have to enter to do an uppercut and things like that?

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:04.740
Yeah. This is just like, okay, I get it.

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:12.220
So like, I think it's like moving in the right direction, but it's still like that alone still feels kind of janky.

00:14:12.220 --> 00:14:23.820
But on a macro level, like the whole game, as far as like, yeah, leveling up the kind of grinding, the kind of RPG elements, the sort of like the, the, the picking your stats and training your thing.

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:29.720
That part, I think is like the, the draw for me that makes me forgive some of the jankiness.

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:34.260
Your, your point about the lack of feedback.

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:46.960
Uh, I, I think it's really funny that I think that there is feedback like after the fact, like I know when I'm hitting the guy, I just don't know when you're supposed to feel that.

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:54.820
Um, because it was some really hilarious situations where it'll just be like 30 seconds of a, of, it looks like a slap fight.

00:14:54.820 --> 00:15:04.360
Like no one is landing a single hit, but we are basically hugging each other and both just punching past each other.

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:06.600
And it's like, is this a loop?

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:12.560
Is this a bug? Um, but yeah, so I totally understand your point.

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:18.420
It, there's no actual feedback that you can act upon during that.

00:15:18.700 --> 00:15:39.760
One thing from a presentation standpoint that they did that I, I appreciated though, is that the camera never changes, but they managed to make the boxers move, like circle each other in an automatic way that doesn't, uh, like require the player to be like, okay, now I have to switch to the B button in this direction to be, you know, turn about face.

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:41.540
It, it just does it naturally.

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:46.640
And it felt natural in the game too, as you're, as you're going around the ring.

00:15:47.040 --> 00:16:01.340
And I, I wasn't able to quite understand how it was working, but it also made it so this way, like, you know, maybe if you were trading blows or something, you just needed to change your angle a little bit or, or step back and not get cornered on the ropes.

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:05.440
Like it communicated that part of boxing pretty well, in my opinion.

00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:26.060
Yeah. Once you start getting farther away from your opponent, the, like the actual, like D pad controls start to feel kind of strange because it, it seems to be instead of like a, uh, uh, like an absolute left, right, up, down, it is sort of like the North star is your opponent.

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:33.000
And as you get farther away, what originally felt kind of like, Oh, I just turn this way to sort of circle him.

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:39.440
Uh, it, it starts to feel kind of counterintuitive, but yeah, you're it's, that's kind of like an outlier situation.

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:54.860
Um, I'm not really sure what being, uh, if there is any actual gameplay difference, uh, is like being on one side or the other or being slightly above or below, but you do at least land your hits when you're slightly above or below,

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:58.860
which not a lot of these games have, uh, mastered.

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:00.880
So that's, that's pretty good.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:18.360
Yeah. I really liked the, the presentation of the boxing fights and, and even just the, you know, going between the rounds and, uh, seeing that they were having them scored and you could see the score in real time of like, you know, 10, six or 10, 10, uh, just kind of knowing where you stand.

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:20.860
Most of these fights don't really go to that though.

00:17:20.860 --> 00:17:26.860
They, they usually become, uh, uh, knockouts or, or, uh, total, uh, what was that?

00:17:26.980 --> 00:17:29.280
TKO was total knockout, technical knockout.

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:33.960
