00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:19.740
Like you, you will have to like raise your stats and, and learn to, you know, fight these guys because they have, uh, higher stamina, which will make them last longer.

00:18:19.740 --> 00:18:22.800
And, uh, they'll most likely knock you out before you get knocked out.

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:24.800
So to make sure you train your stamina, that kind of thing.

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:37.100
I, uh, you know, it doesn't, it doesn't necessarily feel like rock them, sock them robots where you're just like constantly being like hit in the head again and again and again and again, and you can't like escape it.

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:45.580
There, there was like some, some consistency to trading blows or, um, or being able to break away from, uh, each other.

00:18:45.820 --> 00:18:48.680
So I, I think from a boxing perspective, it got all that right.

00:18:48.680 --> 00:19:04.160
Well, and I wonder if you guys have any insight on, on this because I was a little, I had a hard time kind of interpreting or understanding how the blocking works because I did the same thing as you for a while, Mike, where I would like put all of my points into uppercuts or whatever.

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:06.840
And I would just kind of use that over and over again.

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:16.920
But then I, I wasn't sure I figured the reason that wouldn't work consistently would be because I'll do a bunch of uppercuts and then he'll block, you know, his face.

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:18.720
And then I won't be able to do that or whatever.

00:19:19.540 --> 00:19:27.780
But I couldn't tell like when to know where he's blocking or where I'm blocking to know what to use to counter that.

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:32.700
And I feel like that is like, there was like the key missing piece for me to decide which punches to be using.

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:42.600
I was able to block pretty consistently, just sort of randomly selecting a D button to like press while pressing B.

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:50.680
But I had no idea how to do a specific like hook or straight at all, even though I'm sure this is in the manual.

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:53.700
Um, I was not doing very well with it.

00:19:53.700 --> 00:20:01.800
So I was just sort of spreading my points between all of them because I knew it was just going to be the luck of the draw of what actually came out of my character.

00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:17.600
Um, but yeah, I think, I feel like it's just, if they had more telegraphing, which this game, like there, like there's no real telegraph from what the, what your opponent, what their animation is saying before they actually throw whatever punch.

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:20.660
It's kind of hard to know to block one or the other.

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:41.420
Yeah, I think the problem is because that B button just does all the blocking without, um, having like a, you know, directional, like, oh, uh, since a hook shot is, um, uh, is with the left and right D pads that I should, um, that it should be the same to block them.

00:20:41.540 --> 00:20:45.000
Right. I should have to hit like the B button and the left and right on the D pads.

00:20:45.380 --> 00:20:47.920
Uh, it's just this B defensive block.

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:53.020
So you, you, you're not anticipating like, okay, they're currently blocking for a straight.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:54.620
So I should throw an uppercut.

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:55.940
Like that's not happening.

00:20:56.260 --> 00:21:08.140
And so it's missing a little bit of that, uh, rock, paper, scissors mechanic to it of like seeing how your opponent is, is, is preparing for your attack and then tricking them with something else.

00:21:08.140 --> 00:21:17.540
Uh, but yeah, that's basically the, the, maybe the miscommunication there, Joe, is that blocking is just one button and it blocks.

00:21:17.560 --> 00:21:23.180
